Selective laser melting (SLM) builds near-net-shape, complex-geometry metallic components directly from powder by selectively melting and fusing powder layers with a high-power laser; the process can produce near-full-density parts (up to 99.9% relative density) and is applicable to a range of metals due to recent laser developments. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Post-build heat treatments alter the SLM maraging-steel microstructure in multiple, measurable ways: SLM as-fabricated parts can develop extremely fine submicron grains and in situ precipitation (for example, needle-shaped Ni3X nanoprecipitates that produce age hardening), and deliberate heat treatments further change precipitate characteristics, relieve residual stresses, and modify mechanical anisotropy. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}} Heat treatments that approach intercritical temperatures can also induce martensite-to-austenite reversion, producing reversed austenite largely at martensite-lath boundaries. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Compared with the extensive literature on mechanical performance and fatigue, corrosion behaviour of SLM maraging steels is less comprehensively understood: reviews and studies note gaps in understanding of fatigue and other performance metrics for SLM steels, which parallels a limited and still-developing body of work on corrosion. {{cite:S5#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}} Where corrosion-related studies exist, they show that prior heat treatment and microstructural state strongly affect corrosion outcomes—for example, intercritical tempering that produces reversed austenite and local Ni enrichment is reported to enhance pitting resistance, {{cite:S4#c1}} and thermochemical treatments such as plasma nitriding improve wear and corrosion resistance only when combined with appropriate prior heat treatments (nitriding without prior aging can produce cracks in the compound layer). {{cite:S2#c1}}

rows.bRaw:
Selective laser melting builds near net-shape metallic components by selectively melting and fusing powder layers with a high power-density laser, achieving up to 99.9% relative density and enabling economically viable, near–full-density parts with complex geometries. {{cite:S1#c1}} Metal additive manufacturing similarly produces complex engineering parts in a layer-by-layer manner using a high-energy source, reinforcing SLM’s suitability for intricate designs. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Heat treatment after printing modifies the SLM microstructure and properties: in grade 300 maraging steel, the as-fabricated state exhibits an average submicron grain size of 0.31 μm, indicative of cooling rates up to 10^7 K/s, while aging induces massive needle-shaped Ni3X (X = Ti, Al, Mo) nanoprecipitates that drive age hardening via Orowan bowing and coherency strain mechanisms; heat treatments also relieve residual stresses and slightly reduce mechanical anisotropy. {{cite:S3#c1}} Intercritical tempering within the α′ + γ range at 720°C promotes reversed austenite, which nucleates mainly along martensite lath boundaries due to local Ni enrichment from the dissolution of intermetallic particles. {{cite:S4#c1}} This reversed austenite improves corrosion performance in 3.5 wt% NaCl, with the 720°C-tempered condition showing a higher pitting potential, lower corrosion current density, and lower corrosion rate than as-printed, aged, and homogenized specimens without reversed austenite. {{cite:S4#c1}} AM can also act as an intrinsic heat treatment that triggers in situ phase transformations in precipitation-hardened steels, further linking processing history to microstructural evolution. {{cite:S6#c1}}

While reviews have primarily emphasized mechanical properties of SLM parts, corrosion has been addressed through targeted studies. {{cite:S1#c1}} For example, comprehensive evaluations on SLM maraging steel include corrosion resistance alongside impact and tribological performance across as-fabricated and heat-treated states. {{cite:S3#c1}} Plasma nitriding at 520°C for 6 h can enhance wear and corrosion resistance, but nitriding without prior aging leads to cracks in the compound layer, whereas prior heat treatment enables the additive-manufactured steel to benefit from the thermochemical process. {{cite:S2#c1}} Broader assessments of AM steels report that unique microstructures with hierarchical (sub)grains and fine precipitates can enhance corrosion resistance compared to conventional counterparts, even as achieving acceptable ductility and fatigue performance remains challenging. {{cite:S6#c1}} Fatigue behavior, in particular, is identified as an area with significant knowledge gaps relative to other properties and processing parameters in SLM steels. {{cite:S5#c1}}

rows.aRaw:
Mobile health applications have been proposed as a low-cost support for diabetes self-management, a proposition supported by the rapid proliferation of health apps—estimated at no fewer than 40,000 in 2012 and exceeding 100,000 by 2014—which has created a large, accessible supply of tools that could be deployed at low marginal cost compared with face-to-face services. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S4#c1}} 

Randomized and non-randomized trials have sought to quantify the effect of these apps on glycaemic control. Some early and small studies of specific apps (for example, BlueStar) reported reductions in hemoglobin A1c in limited samples, but a multicenter pragmatic randomized controlled trial of BlueStar did not detect a difference between intervention and control arms at 3 months for the primary outcome of HbA1c (mean difference −0.42, 95% CI −1.05 to 0.21; P = .19). {{cite:S6#c1}} An exploratory analysis from that trial did, however, associate greater individual app use with modest improvements in HbA1c: each additional day of app use over the study period corresponded to a $0.016$‑point decrease in 3‑month HbA1c (95% CI −0.03 to −0.003), indicating a dose–response relation between usage intensity and glycaemic outcome among users. {{cite:S6#c1}} 

Mechanisms by which apps are intended to affect glycaemia include promotion of self‑monitoring, immediate feedback on glucose trends, individualized decision support, and behavioral incentives. Self‑monitoring is the most commonly applied behavior‑change technique reported in reviews of app interventions. {{cite:S4#c1}} In pilot work with adolescents using an app that combined automated glucometer data transfer, trend analysis, prompting, social features and gamified rewards, daily blood‑glucose measurement frequency rose by 50% (from 2.4 to 3.6 measurements per day; P = .006, n = 12), illustrating how design features that reduce friction and add incentives can increase proximal self‑management behaviours that plausibly mediate glycaemic benefit. {{cite:S2#c1}} 

Despite these potential mechanisms, engagement commonly declines after the first weeks. Qualitative research identifies multiple barriers to continued use—such as lack of perceived usefulness, burdensome interaction, concerns about credibility and privacy, and mismatch with user needs—and conversely highlights motivators and design elements that sustain use, including ease of use, real‑time feedback, personalization, reminders, goal setting, and credible information. {{cite:S5#c1}} Systematic reviews similarly report heterogeneous study quality and persistent risk of bias, while noting that interventions incorporating real‑time feedback, individualized elements, and health‑professional involvement tend to show stronger behaviour‑change signals. {{cite:S4#c1}} Moreover, pragmatic trials find that contextual factors (for example, clinical site) substantially affect how much participants actually use apps, and low overall usage may explain null average effects on HbA1c in some trials. {{cite:S6#c1}} 

Taken together, the evidence indicates that apps can change self‑management behaviours when they achieve sustained, sufficiently frequent use through features such as automated data transfer, feedback and incentives, but real‑world effectiveness on glycaemic control is inconsistent and appears contingent on user engagement and contextual implementation. {{cite:S2#c1}} {{cite:S6#c1}} {{cite:S4#c1}}
